In JavaScript, besides Promise.all(), there are several other functions for handling multiple promises efficiently:

Promise.allSettled()

Waits for all promises to settle (either fulfilled or rejected).
Returns an array of objects with { status, value } or { status, reason }.

`const promises = [
  Promise.resolve(1),
  Promise.reject("Error"),
  Promise.resolve(3),
];

Promise.allSettled(promises).then(console.log);
`

Promise.race()

Resolves or rejects as soon as the first promise settles.

`
const p1 = new Promise((resolve) => setTimeout(resolve, 100, "A"));
const p2 = new Promise((resolve) => setTimeout(resolve, 50, "B"));

Promise.race([p1, p2]).then(console.log); // "B"
`


Promise.any()

Resolves as soon as one promise fulfills.
Ignores rejected promises and throws an AggregateError if all reject.

`
const p1 = Promise.reject("Error 1");
const p2 = new Promise((resolve) => setTimeout(resolve, 50, "Success"));
const p3 = Promise.reject("Error 2");

Promise.any([p1, p2, p3]).then(console.log).catch(console.error); // "Success"
`
